		{"id":"c942fc2bf7c7f0e1","x":864,"y":160,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"smart contracts"},
		{"id":"b36b4d8266a2cebf","x":29,"y":-500,"width":250,"height":235,"color":"1","type":"text","text":"Wallets serve three main functions to the user:\n1. Secret management\n2. Message Creation\n3. Access Information"},
		{"id":"5d291bb221184157","x":1320,"y":-134,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"value"},
		{"id":"215833895f300f98","x":-416,"y":130,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"hardware wallet"},
		{"id":"f916b06a60eaae8f","x":-760,"y":130,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"USB"},
		{"id":"498654b10406a2ad","x":-760,"y":222,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"QR Code"},
		{"id":"246309325b025854","x":-760,"y":336,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"Bluetooth"},
		{"id":"826f76402dee3c2d","x":-760,"y":436,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"LAN"},
		{"id":"0f4bfcad51fa3c5d","x":-760,"y":40,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"NFC"},
		{"id":"9a50e20befbcce0e","x":-484,"y":-338,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"wallet.dat"},
		{"id":"282938de51f6f2bf","x":864,"y":-260,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"multisig"},
		{"id":"ae4ddf713f9249c2","x":580,"y":-626,"width":728,"height":332,"type":"text","text":"The 'access' portion of the wallet communicates with the 'key management' portion by creating something for it to sign. It does this by understanding the various \"chain languages\" which describe what and how a given asset is to be changed on the blockchain. In some cases, it simply needs to provide proof of ownership of a given key, in which it will ask the 'key management' section to sign a simple message with the associated key. \n\nIf the `key management` section is not reliant upon a hardware wallet to sign messages, then it will assume that the txn that is being sent is formed correctly and checked by the `access` section. A hardware wallet will do another round of verification _on the device's secure display_ to ensure the details of a given transaction are correct. "},
		{"id":"029f0a1f5d5c8d41","x":867,"y":244,"width":250,"height":96,"type":"text","text":"where to send txns and how to know if they're confirmed"},
		{"id":"6c6c3112fb9f6f1b","x":-165,"y":396,"width":691,"height":338,"color":"1","type":"text","text":"Overarching concepts that need to be understood:\n- The \"wallet\" is a misnomer. It doesn't store value. What is stores is access to value (keys), information about where it is, and how to manipulate it."},
		{"id":"7e60e79f11ae0141","x":526,"y":214,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"information infra"},
		{"id":"06cfadda4d1ec238","x":869,"y":378,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"address history"},
		{"id":"8ed9a65cee8ca4d4","x":869,"y":481,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"asset value"},
		{"id":"dff7784173191dcf","x":869,"y":577,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"asset registry (e.g. OpenSea)"},
		{"id":"bbc9223dfcd1df2a","x":866,"y":664,"width":250,"height":70,"type":"text","text":"awaiting messages (waku)"}
	],
